    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nCadillac F1 Team Reportedly Up for Sale and the Asking Price Is $3 Billion\n8 photos\nPhoto: Cadillac F1 Team\nWell, that was fast. You know that old saying about where there's smoke, there's fire? Let's just say that things aren't looking particularly rosy for Mark Walter and his TWG Global group, with federal investigators scrutinizing billions of dollars in transactions involving his insurance businesses. We're talking potentially $20 billion here, which explains why Walter has been busy unloading high-profile sports assets since August.\nFrom the outside, it looks like a deliberate effort to generate liquidity. First, Walter unloaded his controlling stake in the Los Angeles Lakers at a $12.5 billion valuation for the NBA franchise. That happened back in August. Then, in September, he sold his 12.8% stake in Chelsea Football Club. There's word on the street that the LA Sparks are up for grabs too, for around $700 million.\nOn top of that, Walter is reportedly looking to unload his controlling interest in Cadillac F1 Team, seeking around $3 billion for the American outfit. It seems as though the only asset he has no interest in unloading is the Los Angeles Dodgers MLB franchise. I don't blame him. They won back-to-back World Series titles in 2024 and 2025, and they're arguably the best-run franchise in American sports.\nIs there a world where the Dodgers also end up on the auction block? Conceivably, yes. But we'll have to wait and see how things develop.\nBack to Cadillac, the asking price is $3 billion according to three people familiar with the process, and there are at least seven parties interested in purchasing the team, as reported by Sportico . Walter's racing assets also include IndyCar and NASCAR teams.\nWhat's interesting is that while TWG is the controlling owner in Cadillac F1 Team , the overall ownership breakdown remains unknown with General Motors and its Cadillac subsidiary in the mix as well. TWG and GM paid a $450 million fee and invested an additional $1 billion in a team that's on track to lose roughly $400 million this year - according to multiple sources.\nPhoto: Cadillac F1 Team Cadillac has been struggling a great deal in F1 in this maiden season of theirs, which was to be expected. They've yet to score a single point. On the plus side, Sergio Perez recently extended his contract with the team through 2029 (at the very least).\nIs Cadillac F1 Team worth $3 billion? Seeing how the grid average for the 10 teams was $3.42 billion as recently as a year ago, I'd say that yes, that's a fair valuation for late 2026 or 2027 - whenever the reported sale might go through. It would also make the U.S. outfit more valuable, on paper, than Williams, Alpine, Racing Bulls, Kick Sauber and Haas. Nearly twice as valuable as Haas, that is... and about as valuable as Aston Martin.\nMeanwhile, Ferrari tops the charts with a valuation north of $6 billion, which sounds about right.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ncadillac f1 team Cadillac General Motors motorsport Formula 1 F1 report\nAbout the author: Sergiu Tudose\nSergiu got to experience both American and European car \"scenes\" at an early age (his father drove a Ford Fiesta XR2 supermini in the 80s). After spending over 15 years at local and international auto publications, he's starting to appreciate comfort behind the wheel more than raw power and acceleration. Full profile\nUP NEXT This 1956 Ford F-100 With 800 Horsepower Is the Truck Your Grandpa Wished He Had",
